Title:
Morion

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- Morion is a proof-of-concept framework for experimenting with symbolic execution on real-world binaries, currently focused on ARMv7.  
- It solves the practical gap between “pure” symbolic execution and messy firmware/embedded targets by first recording concrete execution traces and then replaying/analyzing them symbolically.  
- The tool uses GDB/GDB-multiarch for tracing (capturing initial register/memory context and executed instruction sequences) and Triton as the symbolic execution engine.  
- It supports analysis passes such as backward slicing, branch/path reasoning for coverage, detection of control-flow and memory-hijack conditions, symbolic function modeling, and assistance for ROP chain generation.  
- It’s useful for exploit developers, firmware/embedded pentesters, and security researchers who want to triage vulnerabilities and derive constraints from observed executions.  
- It’s interesting because it operationalizes trace-based symbolic execution workflows (including remote/cross-platform tracing) rather than requiring full-system emulation.

Technical Focus:
- Trace-based (concolic) symbolic execution with Triton
- GDB-based instruction tracing and context capture (regs/memory)
- Backward slicing and constraint solving (Z3 via Triton)
- Control-flow hijack and memory corruption condition detection
- Branch/path coverage reasoning from traces
- ROP chain assistance/generation for ARMv7

Use Cases:
- Record a crashing/interesting execution on an ARMv7 target and symbolically derive input constraints
- Identify conditions for control-flow hijack (e.g., PC overwrite) or memory hijack from a concrete trace
- Compute backward slices to isolate which inputs influence a sink (branch, write, call)
- Analyze branch feasibility and explore alternative paths starting from a recorded trace
- Assist exploit development by generating/validating ROP chains for ARMv7 firmware

Keywords:
Morion, symbolic execution, concolic execution, Triton, ARMv7, GDB, gdb-multiarch, instruction tracing, concrete trace, backward slicing, path constraints, Z3, Capstone, control-flow hijack, memory corruption, ROP, branch coverage, firmware exploitation, pwndbg